WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:04.145
Once upon a time, there lived a woodcutter.

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:07.157
Every day he went to work in the forest.

3
00:00:07.158 --> 00:00:12.037
One day, after working hard, the woodcutter decided to head home.

4
00:00:12.038 --> 00:00:18.523
He picked up his old wooden ax and began walking.

5
00:00:18.524 --> 00:00:22.223
Soon he heard the sound of rushing water.

6
00:00:22.224 --> 00:00:25.557
"That's strange," he thought.

7
00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:29.218
"I haven't heard that sound here before."

8
00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:34.494
Feeling thirsty, the woodcutter followed the sound.

9
00:00:34.495 --> 00:00:37.896
Through the trees he saw a waterfall.

10
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:42.831
It was falling into a deep pond with clear, sparkling water.

11
00:00:42.832 --> 00:00:45.981
The woodcutter stepped toward the pond.

12
00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:52.248
"Amazing," he thought. "I never knew this pond was here."

13
00:00:52.249 --> 00:00:55.409
The woodcutter crouched to take a drink.

14
00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.460
Suddenly—

15
00:00:58.461 --> 00:01:03.374
splash! He saw several ugly green frogs hop out of the

16
00:01:03.375 --> 00:01:06.509
pond. Then he noticed something

17
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.531
else. His ax was

18
00:01:08.532 --> 00:01:13.858
missing! "Oh no!" He looked around

19
00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:19.548
frantically. "That's my only ax! I need it for

20
00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:22.747
work!" His eyes landed on the

21
00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:26.265
pond. "I must have dropped it in the

22
00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:30.332
water. That was what made the

23
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:34.603
splash." The woodcutter slumped down, holding his head in his

24
00:01:34.604 --> 00:01:36.801
hands. He didn't know how to

25
00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:40.969
swim. And he didn't have enough money to buy a new

26
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:46.765
ax. "How will I work?" he

27
00:01:46.766 --> 00:01:50.221
moaned. "Excuse me," a voice

28
00:01:50.222 --> 00:01:53.702
said. "Something fell into my

29
00:01:53.703 --> 00:01:59.871
pond." In front of him, a fairy was holding a glimmering

30
00:01:59.872 --> 00:02:05.507
ax. "Is this silver ax yours?" the fairy

31
00:02:05.508 --> 00:02:08.820
asked. "No," the woodcutter

32
00:02:08.821 --> 00:02:15.209
said. "My ax fell into the pond, but that one isn't

33
00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:20.178
mine." The fairy slipped silently into the water without making a

34
00:02:20.179 --> 00:02:25.334
ripple. She quickly returned with another magnificent ax.

35
00:02:25.335 --> 00:02:30.357
"Is this gold ax yours?" she asked.

36
00:02:30.358 --> 00:02:33.088
The woodcutter shook his head.

37
00:02:33.089 --> 00:02:39.705
"No, my ax was a simple old ax made of wood."

38
00:02:39.706 --> 00:02:43.101
"I see," the fairy said.

39
00:02:43.102 --> 00:02:48.431
She disappeared into the water again, and reappeared.

40
00:02:48.432 --> 00:02:51.984
"Is this wooden ax yours?"

41
00:02:51.985 --> 00:02:54.921
The woodcutter's face lit up.

42
00:02:54.922 --> 00:03:01.481
"Yes, that's it! May I have it back, please?"

43
00:03:01.482 --> 00:03:04.810
The fairy handed the ax to the woodcutter.

44
00:03:04.811 --> 00:03:07.943
"You are a very honest man.

45
00:03:07.944 --> 00:03:11.479
I would like to reward your honesty.

46
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.981
I will give you the silver and gold axes."

47
00:03:16.982 --> 00:03:23.406
"Thank you," the woodcutter said, delighted with his good fortune.

48
00:03:23.407 --> 00:03:29.387
At home, the woodcutter told his neighbor what had happened.

49
00:03:29.388 --> 00:03:34.525
"Interesting," the neighbor said.

50
00:03:34.526 --> 00:03:39.014
The neighbor grabbed a wooden ax and ran into the forest.

51
00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:44.489
He found the mysterious pond and hurled his ax into the water.

52
00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.193
For a while nothing happened.

53
00:03:47.194 --> 00:03:54.221
Meanwhile some ugly green frogs sat around the pond, staring at him.

54
00:03:54.222 --> 00:03:58.999
"What are you looking at?" the man said.

55
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:05.865
Suddenly the fairy appeared out of nowhere, holding a magnificent silver ax.

56
00:04:05.866 --> 00:04:10.687
"Is this your silver ax?" the fairy asked.

57
00:04:10.688 --> 00:04:18.763
"Yes!" The man nodded eagerly. "I guess I dropped it—"

58
00:04:18.764 --> 00:04:22.327
"Lies!" the fairy snapped.

59
00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:27.462
"This is not your silver ax, and you did not drop it.

60
00:04:27.463 --> 00:04:32.139
You are a liar, and I don't like liars.

61
00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.889
Now I will punish you!"

62
00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:42.424
The man shook with fear. "I'm sorry. Please don't—"

63
00:04:42.425 --> 00:04:48.633
Zap! An ugly green frog now sat in the man's place.

64
00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:51.212
The fairy smiled.

65
00:04:51.213 --> 00:04:54.907
"Nothing good comes from lying.

66
00:04:54.908 --> 00:05:03.619
Honesty is always the best policy!"

